      <section class="capability-opening project-chapter-first" data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="80">
        <h2 id="giving-the-question-answering-assistant-better-knowledge">Knowledge Management</h2>
        <p class="chapter-intro">Giving the assistant useful knowledge, and keeping it current.</p>
        <p>When someone asks why a website still uses an old security certificate, the platform’s assistant retrieves relevant material from its knowledge base and uses it to write an answer. This approach, known as retrieval-augmented generation or RAG, makes the quality of that answer depend on what the library contains.</p>
        <p>The library might explain how to renew a certificate while leaving out how the application starts using the replacement. The assistant would then be trying to answer with only part of the explanation, however well it could write. I built Knowledge Management so these gaps could lead to useful research, with AI also suggesting questions that had yet to be asked. People review the proposed material before it enters the library, and source monitoring keeps track of guidance that changes afterwards.</p>
      </section>

      <section data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="80">
        <h3 id="where-the-assistant-needs-more-knowledge">Where does the assistant need more knowledge?</h3>
        <p>An unanswered question gives us a reason to look for more knowledge, though waiting for people to ask would leave useful gaps undiscovered. I used AI to examine the library and suggest related questions that someone might need help with. A question about renewing a certificate, for example, can point towards another about application code that still refers to the old one.</p>
        <p>Some unasked questions are already covered. Documentation collected for a Python web app may also explain how to host a REST API, so the fact that nobody has asked about the API does not itself call for more research. The matrix separates whether a question has been asked from whether the knowledge can support it. Its two missing cells give us the research inputs, questions raised by people and useful gaps predicted by AI.</p>
        <p>Explore the four cases below to see where more knowledge would help. Ask AI to predict a gap, then watch the two missing cases lead to a search. The source-monitoring tab has a separate example of what happens when accepted guidance changes.</p>
      </section>
      <ItOpsJourney id="certificate-knowledge-journey" class="ops-wide" kind="knowledge" />

      <section data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="80">
        <h3 id="learning-from-the-questions-the-assistant-cannot-answer">Finding the material that will help</h3>
        <p>In the crawler research, I tried starting with a vendor’s documentation URL and letting the crawler explore. It could collect plenty of pages while missing the procedure needed to answer a question, because the website’s organisation did not necessarily match the problem we were trying to understand.</p>
        <p>I used clustering to give related gaps a shared research objective. Certificate expiry, renewal, and a replacement that fails to take effect all belong to the certificate lifecycle. That objective guides the agent’s search for authoritative starting pages and its focused crawling, so useful material can be followed across documentation boundaries.</p>
        <p>The search continues while related pages add useful knowledge, giving the library a better chance of helping with the next question too. Every original gap remains a coverage requirement. If one is still unresolved, the agent looks for another starting page through seed discovery. Once the gaps have evidence and further pages add little, the sources go to a reviewer with an explanation of what each would contribute.</p>
      </section>

      <section data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="80">
        <h3 id="keeping-answers-connected-to-their-sources">What happens when the guidance changes?</h3>
        <p>A vendor can change its renewal requirements after we have accepted its guidance, leaving an explanation that used to be useful out of date. To find the affected knowledge, I keep each source’s original link, an accepted snapshot, and mappings to the passages derived from it. Together, these records form the Source Registry.</p>
        <p>The system revisits those original links and compares the latest content with the saved snapshots. When a page has changed, the agent reads the difference alongside the linked knowledge to judge whether the advice needs to change as well. It can propose a replacement, recommend removing obsolete guidance, or return to discovery for more evidence. An irrelevant difference needs no update, and an unchanged page never reaches the agent. Proposed additions, replacements, and removals go through human review before they are applied.</p>
      </section>

